WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.408
Softmax, worked out.

2
00:00:01.688 --> 00:00:10.392
Softmax maps any real vector z to a probability vector: p_j = exp(z_j) divided by the sum of exp(z_k).

3
00:00:10.672 --> 00:00:12.549
Three properties matter constantly.

4
00:00:12.829 --> 00:00:24.477
It is invariant to adding a constant to every logit (the constant cancels), which is why implementations subtract the maximum logit before exponentiating to avoid overflow.

5
00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:28.000
It preserves order (bigger logit, bigger probability).

6
00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:39.949
And its Jacobian is dp_i/dz_j = p_i times (delta_ij minus p_j), a symmetric matrix equal to diag(p) minus p p transpose.

7
00:00:40.229 --> 00:00:52.965
Compose that Jacobian with the loss L = minus log p_y and the chain rule collapses beautifully: the gradient with respect to the logits is p minus the one-hot vector for the target y.

8
00:00:53.245 --> 00:01:02.504
Read it as a force: push up the logit of the correct character by (1 minus its probability), push down every other logit by its probability.

9
00:01:02.784 --> 00:01:09.632
When the model is confident and right, the gradient vanishes; when it is confident and wrong, the push is nearly 1.

10
00:01:09.912 --> 00:01:16.141
You’ll implement and gradient-check this in the next lesson, and derive it again for whole batches in lesson 6.
